Keep the EditContext buffer, the typed text and the caret in step with the IME.

NativeEditContext mirrors the model line into the browser's EditContext and
turns each textupdate back into a type command. Three places in that round
trip assumed the buffer holds only what this class wrote, which stops being
true as soon as an IME edits it, and each one corrupts text with a keyboard
that reads the buffer back (Gboard on Android).

_updateEditContext replaced [0, length of the previous write) with the new
line. Blink has already applied the IME's edit when textupdate fires, so
every character the IME added survived after the new line: typing "kiwi"
left the buffer "kiwiiwik". A keyboard that recomposes the word under the
caret then types that tail back into the document. The buffer is now
compared with and replaced whole.

_emitTypeEvent copied the text between Monaco's caret and the update range
from the live buffer, at offsets of the text before the edit. Blink has
sometimes applied the edit by then and sometimes not, so the copied prefix
or suffix was shifted whenever the edit changed length. It now copies from
_previousEditContextText, the line last written, which is the text those
offsets describe.

The caret delta was measured from updateRangeStart + e.text.length and
ignored the copied prefix and suffix, so a selection-only update ("" at
[0,0) with the caret past 0, which Gboard sends before committing a
suggestion) put the caret past the IME's selection, and the next delete
removed the wrong characters. It is now measured from the start and length
of the text actually typed.

Not fixed here: Chromium keeps a composition's range when the page moves
the selection during it (crbug 379170477), so a key that moves the caret
while a word composes still writes the recomposed word over the old range.

